For his slide on Branch Divergence, he doesn't consider it but you could have nested branches.

The general rule (as I figured out on my own) is you maintain

Reconvergence-PC    Next-PC     Current Mask

......................................................

Once you hit a branch, the instruction should take as an input the reconvergence point.

First you will have to figure out if you have a divergence or not. That's easy, take the AND of your
current vmask and the new vmask returned and if they don't agree we have a divergence!!

Thereafter it should get a mask from the comparison, now add two entries to your Stack with the same
Reconvergence point, one the mask you received and the other the !mask. But for both these masks, AND them
with your current_mask.

This latter part will ensure you can sustain nested branches

The Reconvergence point is telling the Execution unit to proceed no further than that point.


